  "narrative": {
    "essence": "Bosnia-Herzegovina returns to the World Cup for only the second time — a nation that has existed for barely 30 years, born from one of Europe's most devastating conflicts. Their qualification is a sporting miracle wrapped in a story of resilience that goes far beyond football.",
    "footballIdentity": "Football in Bosnia is identity made visible. Three main ethnic communities — Bosniaks, Serbs, and Croats — found something rare under the Zmajevi crest: a shared team to support. The 2014 World Cup qualification was a breakthrough; the team was filled with European league talent led by Edin Džeko. Now a new generation carries that tradition forward. For many Bosnians, national football is the only thing that crosses ethnic lines.",
    "theMoment": "Bosnia qualifies with a squad built around Bundesliga and Serie A talent, a generation raised in the shadow of the 2014 breakthrough. The country's political structure — the Dayton Agreement that created separate governing entities — still makes governing football complicated. Manager Sergej Barbarez, a Bosnian legend who played in Germany, is trying to create something more unified than the country's political reality. The sport is ahead of the politics.",
    "momentType": "historical"
  },
