    "tacticalNote": "Bosnia play a direct 4-3-3 that leverages physicality and set-piece quality. Demirović's movement pulls defenders while Kolašinac bombs forward from left-back. Pjanić's delivery from dead balls is their biggest weapon in any game situation. They're well-drilled, hard to bully, and capable of punishing the slightest defensive lapse.",
    "strength": "Set pieces and physicality — Bosnia is dangerous at every dead ball and can bully teams who aren't prepared for their physicality.",
    "vulnerability": "Against top teams, they concede too much space between midfield and defense — they're caught too high up the pitch when the press fails."
  },
  "watchFor": [
    "Pjanić's set-piece deliveries — corner and free kick routines are carefully rehearsed and Demirović is an excellent aerial target.",
    "Kolašinac overlapping on the left — when Bosnia wins the ball centrally, the first option is often to play wide left and let him run.",
    "Bosnia's reaction to falling behind — their directness increases when chasing games, which can be a strength or create more space to counter."
  ],
